W: 2. Now the officer who 'listed me was a tall and handsome man
W: He said, "You'll make a sailor lad, so come along my man."
W: My waist bein' tall and slender, my fingers long and thin,
W: Oh, the very soon they learned me, I soon exceeded them.
W: I soon exceed them. I soon exceeded them.
W: Oh, the very soon they learned me, I soon exceeded them.
W:
W: 3. Oh they sent me to bunk, and they sent me to bed.
W: To sleep with the sailor lads, I never was afraid.
W: But takin' off my blue coat, it oft'times made me smile
W: For to think I was a sailor and a maiden all the while.
W: A maiden all the while.  A maiden all the while.
W: For to think I was a sailor and a maiden all the while.
W:
W: 4. They sent me off to London to guard at the Tower,
W: And I'm sure I might have been there until my dyin' hour
W: But a lady fell in love with me, I said I was a maid,
W: So she went unto my captain and my secret she betrayed.
W: My secret she betrayed.  My secret she betrayed.
W: So she went unto my captain and my secret she betrayed.
W:
W: 5. Oh the captain came up to me and asked if it was so.
W: I dare not, I dare not, I dare not say no.
W: "It's a pity we should lose you, such a sailor lad you made.
W: It's a pity we should lose you, such a handsome young maid.
W: Such a handsome young maid. Such a handsome young maid.
W: It's a pity we should lose you, such a handsome young maid."
W:
W: 6. So fare thee well good Captain, you've been so kind to me.
W: And likewise my sailor lads, I'm sorry to part with thee.
W: But if ever the Navy needs a lad a sailor I'll remain,
W: I'll put on my cap and feather and I'll run the riggin' again.
W: I'll run the riggin' again. I'll run the riggin' again.
W: I'll put on my cap and feather and I'll run the riggin' again.
